      "body": [
        {
          "type": "TextualBody",
          "label": "ENA 2643.12 verso",
          "value": "\n<ol><li>it is incumbent upon him not to pass judgment</li>\n<li>basing himself on those who are not well versed in the teachings</li>\n<li>of the traditions of the prophets, peace be upon them; the intricacies of their meanings;</li>\n<li>the rules pertaining to the prayers, the times (<i>awq\u0101t</i>) when they take place, and the portions (<i>fu\u1e63\u016bl</i>) [from the Torah];</li>\n<li>the method of performing the bowings (<i>ruk\u016b\u02bf</i>) and prostrations (<i>suj\u016bd</i>); the distribution of the blessings</li>\n<li>and the reasons for them; the distinction between what is a commandment (far\u1e0d) and</li>\n<li>what is a tradition (<i>sunna</i>), what is mandatory and what is permitted, the fixed order of prayer (<i>al-q\u0101n\u016bn minhu</i>), and what is</li>\n<li>supererogatory. I, one of the disciples of their disciples,</li>\n<li>wish to discuss and explain this matter in such a way</li>\n<li>that it cannot be disputed by the minds of men of understanding of any</li>\n<li>nation. [This discussion], following a preface (<i>muqaddimah</i>) similar to an introduction (madkhal) or</li>\n<li>isagoge, will provide proof of what</li>\n<li>the traditions of the prophets, peace be upon them, have conveyed to us. [I will write] in</li>\n<li>Arabic script and language, to the extent allowed by my command of the language of the</li>\n<li>Arabs, relying on the might, power, success, and aid granted by God.</li>\n<li>The traditions of the prophets have transmitted to us</li></ol>\n",
          "format": "text/html"
        }
      ],
      "motivation": [
        "sc:supplementing",
        "translating"
      ],
      "schema:position": 1
    }
